<Step id="STEP_3_VERIFY">
      <Action>
        Per `AX_VERIFICATION_BEFORE_HANDOFF`, run the phase's exact declared commands, staying
        within `AX_PERMITTED_BASH_COMMANDS`. Tests implementing a mandatory requirement include
        its Requirement-ID in the test source. Implement applicable negative/failure cases, not only
        the happy path. Report command, exit status, and concise result without suppressing errors.
        A successful CLI-owned receipt is evidence to return, not permission to edit phase status,
        DONE, or Handoff. If the tool rejects the transition, return its exact diagnostic to the
        orchestrator; never inspect Gennady source or bundled chunks to reinterpret it. A stop that
        is NOT a simple retry-and-continue routes through `AX_BLOCKER_ESCALATION`'s typed outcome as
        `RETURN_SUMMARY_FORMAT`, evidence attached — never silently downgraded to a plain diagnostic.
      </Action>
    </Step>

<Axiom id="AX_VERIFICATION_BEFORE_HANDOFF">
      Phase cannot emit Handoff without one successful `sdd-verify --task <ticket-path> --phase <PhaseID>`. The CLI, not the phase agent, structurally selects and runs the ladder plus every applicable ticket §5 command verbatim, then atomically writes their exact command/role/exit evidence and Target File state as the phase receipt. Any failure writes no reusable receipt and keeps the phase `[~] IN_PROGRESS` until resolved or escalated.

      **MANDATORY (PROTOCOL) — Canonical Gate Rule:** Ticket §5 commands are CANONICAL and CANNOT be substituted. Diagnostics may run while fixing, but only the canonical phase command can create completion evidence; a hand-written `ver` line is not evidence.

      The canon covers the script's BODY, not just its name in §5. Editing what a §5 script actually runs
      (e.g. rewriting `"typecheck"` in `package.json` so it does less, or points elsewhere) while logging the
      unchanged §5 command name is the same violation — the command stayed canonical on paper while its
      behavior was substituted underneath it. The one exception: `package.json`
      (or the script body) is itself among this phase's Target Files AND the change to that script IS the
      phase's stated Objective (a `bootstrap` / `config` phase whose job is exactly to define or rewire the
      script) — then the edit is legitimate work, not a gate dodge, and MUST be recorded as its own decision
      line in the Execution Log (`<ts> decision <script> rewritten ← <reason>`) before the gate runs.

      `sdd-verify --task <ticket-path> --phase <PhaseID>` is NOT supplemental — it owns STEP_5, including §5 extras (see `AX_PERMITTED_BASH_COMMANDS`). §5 therefore holds only EXTRA commands beyond the standard ladder and never repeats `sdd-verify`.

      Phase kind `doc` typically has no verification — empty section is acceptable.
    </Axiom>

<Contract id="RETURN_SUMMARY_FORMAT">
  JSON-like return to orchestrator:
  ```
  {
    "status": "DONE" | "NEEDS_ORCHESTRATOR",
    "outcome": "CONTINUE" | "CONTEXT_ROTATION" | "RECOVERABLE_TECHNICAL" | "SPEC_GOAL_CONFLICT" | "EXTERNAL_AUTHORITY_REQUIRED" | "TECHNICAL_REPLAN_EXHAUSTED",
    "task_id": "<TSK-NN>",
    "phase_id": "<P<N>>",
    "kind": "<kind>",
    "reason": "<specific observed cause>",
    "attempt": { "current": <N>, "budget": <N> },
    "evidence": ["<repo-relative ref>", ...],
    "technical_plan": { "summary": "<bounded repair>", "task_edits": [], "dag_edits": [], "artifact_edits": [] } | null,
    "artifacts": ["<path>", ...],
    "decisions": ["<key=value>", ...],
    "open": ["<id: text>", ...],
    "deviations": ["<id: text>", ...],
    "handoff": "<ticket#EXECUTION_LOG>"
  }
  ```
  `artifacts` / `decisions` / `open` / `deviations` mirror the **Handoff →** line — orchestrator
  threads `artifacts` / `decisions` / `open` into the next phase's prompt, and collects
  `deviations` across the run for the end-of-cycle batched report (`AX_DEVIATION_SELF_RESOLVE`).
  The worker reports facts; the orchestrator owns technical replan and operator routing. Missing
  `@types/node` or config, `eslint-config-prettier`, a repair-command contract mismatch, a missing
  CREATE target, or a decomposition edge is `RECOVERABLE_TECHNICAL`. A worker does not repair
  Gennady internals for a project failure. Only a proven spec-goal conflict, required external
  authority, or exhausted bounded technical replan uses the corresponding escalation outcome.
</Contract>